167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 118 0 R
/E (Locke holds two views.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 118 0 R
/E (The first view of The State of Nature is that the participants have natural rights and observe natural laws. Natural rights are positive liberties. Natural laws are negative constraints. I have a right to life and so does my neighbour. Because we both enjoy it, we are constrained by natural law from killing each other. So, we live cooperatively. As you might see, this is starkly distinct from Hobbes. Hobbes equates that the State of Nature is identical to the State of War. The first-view-Locke thinks it is a mistaken conflation. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
169 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 118 0 R
/E (Why do you need a government here? To answer that, we may have to know a bit more about his two-stage contract theory. A society is not identical to a government. A society requires universal consent between its participants and it is the first contract. A government only requires majority consent that is why it is the second contract. A government is elected to ensure the preservation of the natural laws. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 118 0 R
/Pg 16 0 R
/A [339 0 R 0]
/K [4]
>>
